NNAAMMEE
     ssnnmmpp — simple SNMP client

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ssnnmmpp ggeett | ggeettnneexxtt | bbuullkkggeett [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _a_g_e_n_t _o_i_d _._._.
     ssnnmmpp wwaallkk | bbuullkkwwaallkk [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _a_g_e_n_t [_o_i_d]
     ssnnmmpp sseett [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _a_g_e_n_t _v_a_r_o_i_d _t_y_p_e _v_a_l_u_e [_v_a_r_o_i_d _t_y_p_e _v_a_l_u_e] ...
     ssnnmmpp ttrraapp [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _a_g_e_n_t _u_p_t_i_m_e _t_r_a_p_o_i_d [_v_a_r_o_i_d _t_y_p_e _v_a_l_u_e] ...
     ssnnmmpp ddff [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _a_g_e_n_t
     ssnnmmpp mmiibbttrreeee [--OO _f_n_s] [_o_i_d _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ssnnmmpp utility is a simple SNMP client.

     The subcommands are as follows:

     ssnnmmpp ggeett [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _a_g_e_n_t _o_i_d _._._.
             Retrieve the varbind for _o_i_d from the _a_g_e_n_t.  If more than one
             _o_i_d is specified, retrieve the varbind for each one.

     ssnnmmpp ggeettnneexxtt [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _a_g_e_n_t _o_i_d _._._.
             Retrieve the varbind that follows _o_i_d from the _a_g_e_n_t.  If more
             than one _o_i_d is specified, retrieve the varbind following each
             one of them.

     ssnnmmpp wwaallkk [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _a_g_e_n_t [_o_i_d]
             Retrieve all the varbinds that are branches of _o_i_d from the
             _a_g_e_n_t.  This uses the ggeettnneexxtt subcommand internally and requests
             a single varbind at a time.  If no _o_i_d is specified, it defaults
             to mib-2 (.1.3.6.1.2.1).

     ssnnmmpp bbuullkkggeett [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _a_g_e_n_t _o_i_d _._._.
             Retrieve the next 10 varbinds following each _o_i_d from the _a_g_e_n_t.
             This command is not available for --vv 11.

     ssnnmmpp bbuullkkwwaallkk [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _a_g_e_n_t [_o_i_d]
             Retrieve all the varbinds from the _a_g_e_n_t that are branches of
             _o_i_d.  This uses the bbuullkkggeett subcommand internally to retrieve
             multiple varbinds at a time.  This command is not available for
             --vv 11.

     ssnnmmpp sseett [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _a_g_e_n_t _v_a_r_o_i_d _t_y_p_e _v_a_l_u_e _._._.
             Set one or more _v_a_r_o_i_d _t_o _a _n_e_w _v_a_l_u_e.  The format of the _v_a_r_o_i_d
             _t_y_p_e _v_a_l_u_e triple is described in _D_a_t_a _t_y_p_e_s, below.

     ssnnmmpp ttrraapp [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _a_g_e_n_t _u_p_t_i_m_e _t_r_a_p_o_i_d [_v_a_r_o_i_d _t_y_p_e _v_a_l_u_e _._._.]
             Send a trap message to the _a_g_e_n_t.  The _u_p_t_i_m_e is specified in
             timeticks (centiseconds) or defaults to the system uptime if an
             empty string is given.  The _t_r_a_p_o_i_d is the identification OID
             used by the trap handler to determine its action.  This command
             is not available for --vv 11.

     ssnnmmpp ddff [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _a_g_e_n_t
             An SNMP based version of the df(1) command.  If no size suffix is
             shown, the sizes are in kilobytes.

     ssnnmmpp mmiibbttrreeee [--OO _f_n_S] [_o_i_d _._._.]
             Dump the tree of compiled-in MIB objects.  If _o_i_d is specified it
             will print the objects in the requested output format if avail‐
             able, or print a warning if the object can't be found.

     The _o_p_t_i_o_n_s are as follows:

     --AA _a_u_t_h_p_a_s_s
             The authentication password for the user.  This will be trans‐
             formed to _l_o_c_a_l_a_u_t_h.  This option is only used by --vv 33.

     --aa _d_i_g_e_s_t
             Set the digest (authentication) protocol.  Options are MMDD55, SSHHAA,
             SSHHAA--222244, SSHHAA--225566, SSHHAA--338844 or SSHHAA--551122.  This option defaults to
             SSHHAA.  This option is only used by --vv 33.

     --CC _a_p_p_o_p_t
             For the bbuullkkggeett, bbuullkkwwaallkk, ddff, and wwaallkk subcommands, set the ap‐
             plication specific _a_p_p_o_p_t options by supplying a string of one or
             more of the following modifier letters:

             cc       For wwaallkk and bbuullkkwwaallkk, disable checking the order of
                     MIBs.  On some devices that return MIBs out of order,
                     this may cause an infinite loop.

             EE _e_n_d_o_i_d
                     For wwaallkk, walk the tree up to but excluding _e_n_d_o_i_d.  The
                     blank before _e_n_d_o_i_d is mandatory.

             hh       For ddff print the output in “human-readable” format.

             II       For wwaallkk, do not fall back to returning the original MIB
                     via a ggeett request.

             ii       For wwaallkk and bbuullkkwwaallkk, always do a ggeett request on the
                     specified _o_i_d first.

             nn_n_o_n_r_e_p
                     For bbuullkkggeett and bbuullkkwwaallkk, Set the non-repeaters field in
                     the request to the non-negative integer _n_o_n_r_e_p.  This
                     causes the first _n_o_n_r_e_p _o_i_d arguments to only return a
                     single MIB instead of _m_a_x_r_e_p.  This value defaults to 0.
                     No blank is allowed before _n_o_n_r_e_p.

             pp       For wwaallkk or bbuullkkwwaallkk, also show a summary of the total
                     variables received.

             rr_m_a_x_r_e_p
                     For bbuullkkggeett, bbuullkkwwaallkk and ddff, set the max-repetitions
                     field in the request to the positive integer _m_a_x_r_e_p.
                     This determines the amount of MIBs to return for each
                     specified OID.  This value defaults to 10.  No blank is
                     allowed before _m_a_x_r_e_p.

             ss _s_k_i_p_o_i_d
                     For wwaallkk or bbuullkkwwaallkk don't include _s_k_i_p_o_i_d or its chil‐
                     dren in the walk output.  The blank before _s_k_i_p_o_i_d is
                     mandatory.

             tt       For wwaallkk, Show how long it took to walk the entire tree.

     --cc _c_o_m_m_u_n_i_t_y
             Set the _c_o_m_m_u_n_i_t_y string.  This option is only used by --vv 11 and
             --vv 22cc and has no default.

     --ee _s_e_c_e_n_g_i_n_e_i_d
             The USM security engine id.  Under normal circumstances this
             value is discovered via snmpv3 discovery and does not need to be
             specified.  This option is only used by --vv 33.

     --EE _c_t_x_e_n_g_i_n_e_i_d
             The snmpv3 context engine id.  Most of the time this value can be
             safely ignored.  This option is only used by --vv 33.

     --KK _l_o_c_a_l_p_r_i_v
             The localized privacy password for the user in hexadecimal format
             (optionally prefixed with a 00xx).  This option is only used by --vv
             33.

     --kk _l_o_c_a_l_a_u_t_h
             The localized authentication password for the user in hexadecimal
             format (optionally prefixed with a 00xx).  This option is only used
             by --vv 33.

     --ll _s_e_c_l_e_v_e_l
             The security level.  Values can be nnooAAuutthhNNooPPrriivv (default),
             aauutthhNNooPPrriivv (requires either --AA or --kk) or aauutthhPPrriivv (requires
             either --XX or --KK in addition to the aauutthhNNooPPrriivv requirements).
             This option is only used by --vv 33.

     --nn _c_t_x_n_a_m_e
             Sets the context name.  Defaults to an empty string.  This option
             is only used by --vv 33.

     --OO _o_u_t_p_u_t
             Set the _o_u_t_p_u_t options by supplying a string of one or more of
             the following modifier letters:

             aa  Print the varbind string unchanged rather than replacing non-
                printable bytes with dots.

             ff  When displaying an OID, include the full list of MIB objects.
                By default only the last textual MIB object is shown.

             nn  Display the OID numerically.

             QQ  Remove the type information.

             qq  Remove the type information and the equal sign.

             SS  Display the MIB name and the type information.  This is the
                default behaviour.

             vv  Only display the varbind value, removing the OID.

             xx  Display the varbind string values as hexadecimal strings.

             The mmiibbttrreeee subcommand may only use the [--ffnnSS] output options; no
             output options are available for ttrraapp.

     --rr _r_e_t_r_i_e_s
             Set the number of _r_e_t_r_i_e_s in case of packet loss.  Defaults to 5.

     --tt _t_i_m_e_o_u_t
             Set the _t_i_m_e_o_u_t to wait for a reply, in seconds.  Defaults to 1.

     --uu _u_s_e_r
             Sets the username.  If --vv 33 is used, this option is required.
             This option is only used by --vv 33.

     --vv _v_e_r_s_i_o_n
             Set the snmp protocol _v_e_r_s_i_o_n to either 11, 22cc or 33.  Currently
             defaults to 33.

     --XX _p_r_i_v_p_a_s_s
             The privacy password for the user.  This will be transformed to
             _l_o_c_a_l_p_r_i_v.  This option is only used by --vv 33.

     --xx _c_i_p_h_e_r
             Sets the cipher (privacy) protocol.  Options are DDEESS and AAEESS.
             This option defaults to AAEESS.  This option is only used by --vv 33.

     --ZZ _b_o_o_t_s,_t_i_m_e
             Set the engine boots and engine time.  Under normal circumstances
             this value is discovered via snmpv3 discovery and does not need
             to be specified.  This option is only used by --vv 33.

     The syntax for the _a_g_e_n_t argument is [_p_r_o_t_o_c_o_l:]_a_d_d_r_e_s_s, with the follow‐
     ing format:

           _p_r_o_t_o_c_o_l        _a_d_d_r_e_s_s
           uuddpp | ttccpp       _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e[:_p_o_r_t] | _I_P_v_4_-_a_d_d_r_e_s_s[:_p_o_r_t]
           uuddpp66 | ttccpp66     _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e[:_p_o_r_t] | [[_I_P_v_6_-_a_d_d_r_e_s_s]][:_p_o_r_t] |
                                      _I_P_v_6_-_a_d_d_r_e_s_s:_p_o_r_t
           uunniixx            _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e

     The default _p_r_o_t_o_c_o_l is uuddpp and the default _p_o_r_t is 161, except for the
     ttrraapp subcommand, which uses 162.  uuddppvv66 and uuddppiippvv66 are aliases for uuddpp66;
     ttccppvv66 and ttccppiippvv66 for ttccpp66.  To specify an IPv6-address without a _p_o_r_t,
     the _I_P_v_6_-_a_d_d_r_e_s_s must be enclosed in square brackets.  If the square
     brackets are omitted, the value after the last colon is always inter‐
     preted as a _p_o_r_t.

   DDaattaa ttyyppeess
     Additional data sent to the server is formatted by specifying one or more
     triples of _v_a_r_o_i_d, _t_y_p_e, and _v_a_l_u_e.  Supported types are:

           aa  An IPv4 Address.

           bb  A bitstring.  A list of individual bit offsets separated by
              comma, space or tab.  Must be supplied as a single argument.

           cc  A counter32.

           dd  A decimal string.  A list of individual bytes in decimal form
              separated by space or tab.

           ii  An integer.

           nn  A null object.

           oo  An OID.

           ss  A regular string.

           tt  Timeticks in centiseconds.

           uu  Unsigned integer.

           xx  A hex string.  Similar to a decimal string, but in hexadecimal
              format.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     LC_CTYPE  The character encoding locale(1) used for output.  It decides
               whether objects having a display format of UTF-8 are printed as
               UTF-8, and whether each byte invalid according to the object's
               display format is printed as a UTF-8 replacement character
               (‘�’).

               If unset or set to "C", "POSIX", or an unsupported value, for
               objects having a display format of UTF-8, each _p_r_i_n_t_a_b_l_e non-
               ASCII character is replaced with a single dot (‘.’).  Each byte
               invalid according to the object's display format is printed as
               a question mark (‘?’).

               Each non-printable character is always replaced with a single
               dot (‘.’).

